\subsection{Literature, source context and scope}
The full original catalogue body and source qualifications are preserved above. The relevant primary workshop passages were read in the cached publisher report. Historical claims and subsequent attributed results are distinguished from the mathematical work below. A restricted example, standard lemma or known theorem is not counted as new progress on the entire original target. Missing assumptions and repeated or rhetorical extracts are not silently turned into different solved problems.

\subsection{A complete scalar barrier calculation and its extension gap}
The source asks for every admissible unknown-order plant, with only a relative-degree upper bound known. We test the simplest permitted scalar subclass
\[
\dot y=ay+\beta u,\qquad a\in\mathbb R,\quad\beta>0.
\]
This is a first-order minimum-phase plant. For example $b=\beta,c=1$ gives Rosenbrock determinant $-\beta\ne0$. Set $u=-ky$ with $k=(1-\varphi^2y^2)^{-1}$, so $\gamma_1(k,y)=ky$ is polynomial and no controller state is needed. Use the continuous Lipschitz representative of the prescribed $W^{1,\infty}$ function.
\begin{proposition}
For every scalar plant just specified, every finite initial value and every source-admissible $\varphi$, this feedback has a global solution satisfying $\varphi(t)|y(t)|<1$ for all $t\ge0$. The controller does not need to know $a$ or $\beta$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The right-hand side is continuous in time and locally Lipschitz in $y$ on the open funnel, giving a unique local solution. Write $w=\varphi^2y^2$. While $w<1$, $k\ge1$ and the scalar equation implies $|y(t)|\le |y(0)|e^{a_+t}$, where $a_+=\max(a,0)$; this follows directly by integrating the coefficient $a-\beta k$, with the zero solution handled separately. If $L=\|\varphi'\|_\infty$, then $\varphi(t)\le Lt$ because $\varphi(0)=0$. Choose $\delta>0$ so small that $L^2\delta^2|y(0)|^2e^{2a_+\delta}<1/2$. Thus $w<1/2$ up to $\delta$ wherever the solution exists. On this interval $k\le2$ and $y$ is bounded, so local continuation extends the solution to $\delta$.
Positivity, continuity and the positive limit inferior of $\varphi$ imply
$m=\inf_{t\ge\delta}\varphi(t)>0$: use a compact interval up to a time after which the limit-inferior bound applies. Set $M=a_++L/m$. Almost everywhere for $t\ge\delta$ within the funnel,
\[
\dot w=2\left(\frac{\varphi'}{\varphi}+a-\frac{\beta}{1-w}\right)w
\le2\left(M-\frac{\beta}{1-w}\right)w.
\]
Choose $q\in(w(\delta),1)$ with $\beta/(1-q)>M+1$. Whenever $w\ge q$, its derivative is at most $-2w$. The positive part $(w-q)_+$ is absolutely continuous and has nonpositive derivative almost everywhere, starting at zero; hence it stays zero. Therefore $w\le q<1$ after $\delta$. In particular $k\le(1-q)^{-1}$ and $|y|\le\sqrt q/m$. The solution stays a fixed distance from the funnel singularity and remains bounded, so the local differential equation continues for all time. Every choice of constants in this proof is for analysis only; the feedback formula contains none of them.
\end{proof}
The attempted extension to relative degree two loses the scalar inward-pointing inequality. For $\dot y=v,\dot v=u$ at a time when $\varphi=1$ and $\varphi'=0$, the state $(y,v)=(1/2,100)$ has $\dot w=2yv=100$ independently of the instantaneous $u$. This does not prove that a dynamic controller cannot work; it identifies the missing control of unmeasured derivatives in this proof.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The scalar derivative bounds and the double-integrator counterstate were checked symbolically in the local computation. For instance $M=3,\beta=1,w=q=9/10$ gives upper derivative $-63/5$. No controller for unknown order or arbitrary relative-degree bound was constructed. The source's index range beginning at $i=1$ and its scalar/vector funnel mismatch are retained, not repaired by this scalar lemma. This is a standard restricted funnel argument, with no new original-target completion claimed.

